from lib import *
S='social-science'
lesson(10,S,'history-outbreak-of-world-war-i-and-its-aftermath','causes-and-course-of-world-war-i',"Causes, course and results of World War I","A spark in Sarajevo","On 28 June 1914 a young Serbian nationalist shot the heir to the Austrian throne in Sarajevo. Within weeks Europe was at war. The shot was only the spark - the fuel had been piling up for decades.",
["Long-term causes: militarism (an arms race), the alliance system, imperial rivalry for colonies and markets, and aggressive nationalism. @ long-term causes",
 "Europe was split into two camps: the Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy) and the Triple Entente (Britain, France, Russia). @ two alliance blocs",
 "The immediate cause was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo on 28 June 1914. @ Sarajevo 1914",
 "Austria declared war on Serbia, Russia backed Serbia, Germany backed Austria, and Britain joined when Germany invaded Belgium. @ chain of declarations",
 "The war was fought on the Western Front in trenches, on the Eastern Front and at sea; new weapons such as machine guns, poison gas, tanks and submarines were used. @ trench warfare",
 "The entry of the United States in 1917 and Russia's withdrawal after the 1917 revolution changed the balance; the Allies won and an armistice was signed on 11 November 1918. @ 1917 and 1918",
 "The Treaty of Versailles (1919) punished Germany with war guilt, loss of territory and reparations, and the League of Nations was formed to keep peace. @ Versailles and League"],
("Why is the assassination at Sarajevo called the immediate cause and not the only cause?",[("It triggered the declarations of war in July 1914.","It was the event that set things off."),("But the alliance system turned a local quarrel into a general war.","Alliances pulled other countries in."),("Militarism and imperial rivalry had already built up tension, so war was likely.","These are the long-term causes.")]),
("List the causes of World War I and two results.",["Militarism, alliances, imperialism, nationalism","Sarajevo assassination","Treaty of Versailles and League of Nations"],["Naming only Sarajevo as the cause"]),
["Long-term causes: militarism, alliances, imperialism, nationalism.","Immediate cause: Sarajevo, 28 June 1914.","War ended with the armistice of 11 November 1918.","Versailles Treaty 1919; League of Nations formed."],
["m|The immediate cause of World War I was|the assassination at Sarajevo|the Russian Revolution~It came during the war;the Treaty of Versailles~It ended the war|28 June 1914.",
 "t|Britain entered the war after Germany invaded Belgium.|True|Britain had promised to defend Belgium's neutrality.",
 "n|The year the war began|1914|Archduke Franz Ferdinand was killed in June 1914.",
 "m|The Triple Entente consisted of|Britain, France and Russia|Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy~That was the Triple Alliance;Japan, China and India~Not members|Standard grouping.",
 "t|The League of Nations was formed after World War I.|True|It was meant to maintain peace.",
 "t|The armistice was signed in 1919.|False|The armistice was in November 1918 and the Treaty of Versailles in 1919.",
 "n|The year the United States entered the war|1917|Entry in April 1917.",
 "s|The treaty of 1919 that punished Germany is the Treaty of ____.|Versailles|Signed at the Palace of Versailles."])
lesson(10,S,'civics-indian-constitution','preamble-rights-and-duties',"The Preamble, Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles","The promise at the front","Every Indian school begins its day with the Preamble: 'We, the people of India...'. It is the opening promise of the Constitution and tells us what kind of country India wants to be.",
["The Constitution of India was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950, which we celebrate as Republic Day. @ 26 January 1950",
 "The Preamble declares India to be a sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic republic and promises justice, liberty, equality and fraternity. @ Preamble",
 "Fundamental Rights are the basic rights of every citizen: right to equality, right to freedom, right against exploitation, right to freedom of religion, cultural and educational rights, and right to constitutional remedies. @ six Fundamental Rights",
 "Right to constitutional remedies lets a person go to the High Court or Supreme Court if a right is violated, so Dr Ambedkar called it the heart and soul of the Constitution. @ constitutional remedies",
 "Directive Principles of State Policy are guidelines for the government to build a welfare state; they are not enforceable in court. @ Directive Principles",
 "Fundamental Duties (Article 51A) are duties of citizens, such as respecting the Constitution, the national flag and anthem, protecting the environment and developing scientific temper. @ Fundamental Duties",
 "The Constitution has a Preamble, many Articles grouped in Parts, and Schedules; it can be amended by Parliament by a defined procedure. @ structure"],
("A person's property is wrongly taken by the government without law. Which right helps them, and where do they go?",[("Right to constitutional remedies protects other fundamental rights.","It makes rights enforceable."),("They can file a petition in the High Court or the Supreme Court.","These courts issue writs."),("The court can order the government to correct the wrong.","That is how a right is enforced.")]),
("What are Fundamental Rights and how do they differ from Directive Principles?",["Six Fundamental Rights","Enforceable by courts","Directive Principles are guidelines, not enforceable"],["Thinking all rights are unlimited"]),
["The Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950.","The Preamble: sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic republic.","Six Fundamental Rights are enforceable in courts.","Directive Principles guide the government; duties guide citizens."],
["n|How many Fundamental Rights are there?|6|Equality, freedom, against exploitation, religion, cultural and educational, constitutional remedies.",
 "t|Directive Principles of State Policy can be enforced in a court.|False|They are guidelines.",
 "m|The Constitution came into force on|26 January 1950|26 November 1949~That is when it was adopted;15 August 1947~Independence Day|Republic Day.",
 "t|The Preamble promises justice, liberty, equality and fraternity.|True|These are its ideals.",
 "m|Dr Ambedkar called which right the heart and soul of the Constitution?|right to constitutional remedies|right to equality~Important but not so named;right to property~Not a fundamental right now|It enforces all others.",
 "t|Fundamental Duties are listed in Article 51A.|True|They were added in 1976.",
 "n|India became a republic in the year|1950|26 January 1950.",
 "s|India is a sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic ____.|republic|From the Preamble."])
lesson(10,S,'geography-india-location-relief-and-drainage','physical-divisions-and-rivers',"India: location, physical divisions and rivers","India on the globe","India lies in the northern and eastern hemispheres, with the Himalayas in the north and ocean on three sides. Few countries have so many kinds of landscape within their borders.",
["India extends from about 8 degrees 4 minutes N to 37 degrees 6 minutes N latitude, and the Tropic of Cancer passes almost through the middle of the country. @ latitude and Tropic of Cancer",
 "The Indian Standard Meridian is 82.5 degrees E, so Indian Standard Time is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time. @ IST 82.5 E",
 "The physical divisions are the Northern Mountains (the Himalayas), the Northern Plains, the Peninsular Plateau, the Indian Desert, the Coastal Plains and the Islands. @ physical divisions",
 "The Himalayas are young fold mountains with three parallel ranges: Himadri, Himachal and Shiwaliks. @ Himalayan ranges",
 "The Northern Plains were built by the alluvium of the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra rivers and are fertile. @ Northern Plains",
 "Himalayan rivers such as the Ganga and Brahmaputra are perennial because snow melts feed them; Peninsular rivers such as the Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri depend on rain and are seasonal. @ Himalayan vs Peninsular rivers",
 "The Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats meet near the Nilgiris; the Deccan Plateau lies between them. @ Ghats"],
("Why is the Ganga flowing all year while the Godavari swells mainly in the monsoon?",[("The Ganga is fed both by rain and by melting Himalayan snow.","Snowmelt keeps the flow in summer."),("The Godavari is a Peninsular river that depends mainly on monsoon rain.","It has no snow source."),("So Himalayan rivers are perennial and Peninsular rivers are seasonal.","This explains the difference.")]),
("Name the physical divisions of India and contrast Himalayan with Peninsular rivers.",["Six physical divisions","Himalayan rivers are perennial","Peninsular rivers are seasonal"],["Thinking all Indian rivers flow all year"]),
["Latitudes 8N to 37N; Tropic of Cancer near the middle.","IST is based on 82.5 degrees E.","Six physical divisions.","Himalayan rivers: perennial; Peninsular: seasonal."],
["m|The Tropic of Cancer passes through|the middle of India|the north only~Far from it;the south only~Kanyakumari is further south|It crosses several states.",
 "t|The Himalayas are young fold mountains.|True|Formed by plate collision.",
 "n|Number of main physical divisions of India (as listed)|6|Mountains, plains, plateau, desert, coast, islands.",
 "t|The Godavari is a Himalayan river.|False|It is a Peninsular river.",
 "m|Indian Standard Time is based on|82.5 degrees E|0 degrees~That is Greenwich;90 degrees E~Not used|Passes near Mirzapur.",
 "t|The Northern Plains are formed by alluvium.|True|Deposited by rivers.",
 "t|The Eastern and Western Ghats meet near the Nilgiris.|True|The Nilgiri hills.",
 "s|The lowest range of the Himalayas is the ____.|Shiwaliks|South of the Himachal range."])
lesson(10,S,'economics-gross-domestic-product-and-its-growth-an-introduction','gdp-and-sectors',"GDP, per capita income and the three sectors","Counting a nation's output","If every farmer, factory and shop in India counted what it made in a year and we added it all, we would get the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) - a measure of how big the economy is.",
["GDP is the total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in one year. @ GDP",
 "Only final goods are counted, not intermediate goods, to avoid counting the same thing twice (double counting). @ final goods only",
 "Per capita income = national income / population; it shows the average income per person. @ per capita income",
 "The economy has three sectors: primary (agriculture, mining), secondary (manufacturing, construction) and tertiary or services (banking, transport, education). @ three sectors",
 "As an economy develops, the share of the primary sector falls and that of the tertiary sector rises. @ structural change",
 "GDP can be measured at current prices or at constant prices; real GDP (constant prices) removes the effect of inflation. @ real and nominal GDP",
 "GDP growth rate shows how fast the economy is expanding; it does not show how income is shared or the state of the environment. @ growth rate and limits"],
("A state has a national income of Rs 4 000 crore and a population of 2 crore. Find its per capita income.",[("Per capita income = national income / population.","Use the formula."),("= 4 000 crore / 2 crore.","The unit crore cancels."),("= Rs 2 000 per person.","Divide 4 000 by 2.")]),
("What is GDP and why do we count only final goods?",["Total value of final goods and services","Avoid double counting","Per capita income = income / population"],["Counting intermediate goods as well"]),
["GDP = value of final goods and services in a year.","Per capita income = national income / population.","Sectors: primary, secondary, tertiary.","Real GDP removes inflation."],
["m|Banking belongs to the|tertiary sector|primary sector~Agriculture and mining;secondary sector~Manufacturing|It is a service.",
 "t|Intermediate goods are counted in GDP.|False|Only final goods are counted.",
 "t|Agriculture belongs to the primary sector.|True|It uses natural resources directly.",
 "n|Per capita income when income is 1000 and population is 10|100|1000/10.",
 "t|Real GDP is adjusted for inflation.|True|It uses constant prices.",
 "m|Which sector has grown fastest as India developed?|tertiary|primary~Its share has fallen;secondary~Grown but slower|Services.",
 "n|Number of sectors in the economy|3|Primary, secondary and tertiary.",
 "s|Income per person is called ____ income.|per capita|National income divided by population."])
